The game started well: I found a coaligned altar on the second level. I 
sacrificed a couple of monsters for luck and headed into the mines. On the
bottom level, my cat stepped on a polymorph trap and became a queen bee. My
first thought was that it would get killed fairly quickly. Not so: it made it
all the way to the quest. I got a major shock when it killed the priest on the
mall level; queen bees are nasty -- They frequently kill things with one hit
due to poison.

After picking up the luckstone from the mines, I returned to the Dungeons of
Doom. Two levels down I found a bag of tricks and headed back up to the altar.
I maxxed out my luck by sacrificing, and received Mjollnir from Odin. I then
began revisiting all the fountains I had seen so far to see if I could get a
wish through quaffing. I got one wish this way -- gauntlets of power, so I
could throw Mjollny. IMHO, this is one of the best combinations in the game.

After my pet killed a shopkeeper, I returned to the mall level to see if I
could convince it to kill the ones there. No luck; they were all too powerful.
While I was there, I made a mistake: I kicked in a locked door. The guards all
descended on me (no problem), and Odin became displeased. I decided to convert
the altar (since the priest was gone), and sacrifice until he became happy
again. To my surprise, rather than converting the altar to Odin, I converted
myself to Loki. I remained chaotic through the rest of the game.

Before leaving the mall level, I sacrificed for a while to make certain that
my luck was high and my new god was happy. On my way out, I killed an orc
and decided to make one last sacrifice. "An object appears at your feet!"
Stormbringer. I kept it for the whole game, but never used it much. Mjollnir
is a far better weapon, and I had acquired shock resistance somewhere, so the
occasional blast of power wasn't more than a minor nuisence.

The quest was easy. When I reached the final level, I was quaking in my (speed)
boots, since my roommate had been telling me about how nasty Lord Surtur was.
I stepped into the path into Surtur's lair..."The fire giant zaps a wand of
death at you!" Missed. I love luckstones. I stepped out of the way, exchanged
my amulet of ESP for an amulet of reflection, and advanced on him. He tossed
another shot at me, it bounced, he died. I never fought Lord Surtur; I just
picked up the wand and nailed him with it.

I suppose I could have cursed the bell (it did become cursed at some point) and
summoned endless wraiths with it, but I never did.

I lost six points of intrinsic armor class from hitting 'p' when I meant to
hit 'o'. Loki became displeased, and nothing I could do would mollify him.
Solution: a cursed scroll of genocide quickly written up with a magic marker,
and I had plenty of gray dragons to offer him, with some GDSM in the bargen.

The Medusa level was a problem: I couldn't get across the water! I finally
got lucky when praying, and Loki gave me a spellbook of levitation.

The castle was cake.I couldn't figure out how to open the drawbridge with a
bugle, so I zapped a wand of striking at it from a safe distance. Then I
repeatedly threw Mjollnir at the monsters milling around the exit until they
all died. Yawn.

The castle wand was a nice chance to get some much-needed AC. I wished for an
amulet of life saving, just in case of trouble. I found two more, and never
needed any of them. My next wish was a ring of levitation; I would hate to try
to win without one. I got a shield of reflection, since I couldn't wear the
amulet of reflection any more. A +3 Hawaiian shirt added some to my AC; I
shouldn't have bothered -- I lost it in a polymorph a bit later. I finally
settled on 3 cursed scrolls of genocide as my last wish -- just in case.

Hell was tedious -- lots of mazes. I had heard that minotaurs were hard, but
never found that to me the case. The most annoying things I fought were master
lichs -- they kept teleporting away before I could kill them. Halfway through,
I used the Orb to level teleport up to a priest and offer a pile of gold to
him. I had no real troubles in acquiring book and candalabra, picked up the
Amulet, and left.

Going up was no real problem -- I put on a ring of levitation to keep myself
from being teleported down when I went up, and used wands of digging to clear
an easy path. Contrary to my expectations, I found I could teleport while
holding the Amulet, and used this to good effect.

The endlevel was a major pain. The earth, fire, and air planes were tedious
but not too bad, but the water plain was next to impossible. Next time, I'm
wishing for some scrolls of gold detection.

IMHO, the game gets too easy after a while. By the time you make it down to the
castle, you are resistant to everything in existance. Nothing hurts you. At the
end, I was killing archons without a thought. The real challenge in this game
is surviving the beginning.

My advice to new players: stock up on holy water, and get a helm of telepathy.
The helm is one of the most useful things in the game, IMHO. It lets you see
things before they get to you, without putting on and taking of a blindfold
that can become cursed. Very useful in the maze levels.
